},{"id":"a8aaf28e16cb","v":"npnf.basilius.epistulae","w":"basilius.epistulae","a":"basilius_von_casarea","s":236,"p":[5,5],"r":[7,13],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Is. xlii. 6, and 2 Kings vii. 13."},{"id":"da18647f320a","v":"npnf.gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","w":"gregor_nyssa.refutatio_eunomii","a":"gregor_v_nyssa","s":1,"p":[83,83],"r":[23,5],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"For Aseroth perhaps Mazaroth should be read. Cf. Job xxxviii. 32, \"Canst thou lead forth the Mazaroth in their season?\" (R.V.) and 2 Kings xxiii. 5, \"to the planets ( toij mazourwq"},{"id":"246ab98e69ec","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_spiritu_sancto","w":"ambrosius.de_spiritu_sancto","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":25,"p":[2,2],"r":[3,17],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"It has been thought well in translating this verse to keep the words \"spirit\" and \"breath\" as suiting the argument of St. Ambrose. But there can be little doubt that the ordinary translation is the correct one. Bp. Westcott has the following note: \"In Hebrew, Syriac, and Latin the words [for spirit and wind] are identical, and Wiclif and the Rhemish version keep \"spirit\" in both cases, after the Latin. But at present the retention of one word in both places could only create confusion, since the separation between the material emblem and the power which it was used to describe is complete. The use of the correlative verb ( pnei pneuma ), and still at the same time the whole of the phraseology is inspired by the higher meaning. Perhaps also the unusual word ( pneuma , 1 Kings xviii. 45; 1 Kings xix. 11; 2 Kings iii. 17) is employed to suggest this. The comparison lies between the obvious physical properties of the wind and the mysterious action of that spiritual influence to which the name \"spirit,\" \"wind,\" was instinctively applied. The laws of both are practically unknown, both are unseen, the presence of both is revealed in their effects.\"-Westcott on S. John iii. 8."},{"id":"498492c1981b","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_fide","w":"ambrosius.de_fide","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":19,"p":[1,1],"r":[9,30],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Fucus, the word used by St. Ambrose, denoted face-paint in general, but it seems to have also had the especial meaning of a red pigment, or rouge for the cheeks. The custom of face-painting was known of old in the East (2 Kings ix. 30; Ezek. xxiii. 40), whence, most probably, it passed into Greece-it was known, in Ionia at least, when the Odyssey was written (say 900 b.c.)-and thence to Rome. See Dict. Antiq. art. \"Fucus.\""},{"id":"2f6991691da9","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_fide","w":"ambrosius.de_fide","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":42,"p":[5,5],"r":[4,34],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 34."},{"id":"7d52e306724a","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_fide","w":"ambrosius.de_fide","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":42,"p":[5,5],"r":[13,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Rom. viii. 3. Note \"in the likeness of sinful flesh,\" not \"in sinful flesh.\" Cf. Phil. ii. 7; for the miracle referred to, see 2 Kings xiii. 21."},{"id":"47c1092d1f35","v":"npnf.ambrosius.contra_auxentium","w":"ambrosius.contra_auxentium","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":1,"p":[11,11],"r":[6,16],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings vi. 16."},{"id":"4eb1bd027f26","v":"lf.ambrosius.epistulae","w":"ambrosius.epistulae","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":17,"p":[7,7],"r":[6,18],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings. vi. 18."},{"id":"24f3e870f2cc","v":"lf.ambrosius.epistulae","w":"ambrosius.epistulae","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":17,"p":[10,10],"r":[2,4],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 4."},{"id":"df11a2eb3a8d","v":"csel.ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","w":"ambrosius.explanatio_psalmorum","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":10,"p":[10,10],"r":[2,11],"k"