t_libero_arbitrio","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":43,"p":[0,0],"r":[14,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xiv. 10."},{"id":"43063dd4f412","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_sermone_domini","w":"augustinus.de_sermone_domini","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":7,"p":[0,0],"r":[2,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Others follow Augustin in regarding the connection of this verse and the next with the preceding one as very close. All the more must they refuse to yield to persecution, as they have a function in the world which is well represented by salt and light (Weizsäcker, Meyer, etc.). The function of salt is to preserve and to season. With it Elisha healed the unwholesome water (2 Kings ii. 21). The use of salt in the sacrifices is, no doubt, alluded to (Tholuck). It becomes savourless. Dr. Thomson says ( Land and Book , ii. 43), \"It is a well-known fact that the salt in this country (gathered from the marshes in dry weather), when in contact with the ground, or exposed to air and sun, does become insipid and useless.\" The disciples are appointed to communicate the truth and moral grace, before spoken of in the Beatitudes, to counteract the error and corruption m the earth. \"Earth\" not to be confined to \"society as then existing, the definite form the world then presented\" (Lange), but to mankind in general, as Augustin below. \"Wherewith shall it be salted\" does not imply that those who have once fallen cannot be reclaimed (Alford). The comment of Grotius is good: \" Ipsi emendare alios debebent, non autem exspectare ut ab aliis ipsi emendarentur \" (\"They ought to improve others, not expect to be themselves improved by others\")."},{"id":"784c4a5aeb1b","v":"npnf.augustinus.de_sermone_domini","w":"augustinus.de_sermone_domini","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":21,"p":[2,2],"r":[1,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings i. 10."},{"id":"4108833f705d","v":"npnf.augustinus.sermones_nt","w":"augustinus.sermones_nt","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":86,"p":[5,5],"r":[4,29],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 29."},{"id":"7f20906e468c","v":"npnf.augustinus.in_iohannem","w":"augustinus.in_iohannem","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":74,"p":[2,2],"r":[2,9],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 9."},{"id":"a7a0554f9ab7","v":"npnf.augustinus.in_iohannem","w":"augustinus.in_iohannem","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":91,"p":[2,2],"r":[4,35],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 35."},{"id":"41f0fc8c951b","v":"npnf.augustinus.in_iohannem","w":"augustinus.in_iohannem","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":91,"p":[2,2],"r":[13,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xiii. 21."},{"id":"37662cef2972","v":"npnf.augustinus.in_iohannem","w":"augustinus.in_iohannem","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":91,"p":[2,2],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 11."},{"id":"4dddcfe0d7b3","v":"npnf.augustinus.in_iohannem","w":"augustinus.in_iohannem","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":124,"p":[2,2],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 11."},{"id":"c29e89c636ec","v":"npnf.athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","w":"athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":14,"p":[4,4],"r":[20,18],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xx. 18; Is. xxxix. 7."},{"id":"71b3fb4f8d92","v":"npnf.athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","w":"athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":23,"p":[2,2],"r":[5,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings v. 8, 2 Kings v. 15."},{"id":"c59f19451d40","v":"npnf.athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","w":"athanasius.orationes_contra_arianos","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":23,"p":[2,2],"r":[5,15],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings v. 8, 2 Kings v. 15."},{"id":"ccc3c1d7fce7","v":"npnf.athanasius.apologia_contra_arianos","w":"athanasius.apologia_contra_arianos","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":6,"p":[48,48],"r":[9,35],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Dogs without owners, and almost in a wild state, abound, as is well known, in Eastern cities; vid. Psalm lix. 6, Psalm lix. 14, Psalm lix. 15. 2 Kings ix. 35, 2 Kings ix. 36. and for the view taken in Scripture of dogs, vid. Bochart, Hieroz. ii. 56 [and Dict. Bib. s.v.]."},{"id":"67e8dd44784f","v":"npnf.athanasius.apologia_contra_arianos","w":"athanasius.apologia_contra_arianos","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":6,"p":[48,48],"r":[9,36],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Dogs without owners, and almost in a wild state, abound, as is well known, in Eastern cities; vid. Psalm lix. 6, Psalm lix. 14, Psalm lix. 15. 2 Kings ix. 35, 2 Kings ix. 36. and for the view taken in Scripture of dogs, vid. Bochart, Hieroz. ii. 56 [and Dict. Bib. s.v.]."},{"id":"9cd06ffca5fe","v":"npnf.athanasius.apologia_de_fuga","w":"athanasius.apologia_de_fuga","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":20,"p":[0,0],"r":[1,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings i. 10."},{"id":"6f81bc35a046","v":"npnf.athanasius.historia_arianorum","w":"athanasius.historia_arianorum","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":5,"p":[11,11],"r":[6,16],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings vi. 16."},{"id":"24c4528fd76e","v":"npnf.athanasius.vita_antonii","w":"athanasius.vita_antonii","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":1,"p":[2,2],"r":[3,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Cf 2 Kings iii. 11: the expression merely refers to personal attendance (contrast §§47, 93). The text is uncertain, as some mss., both Greek and Latin read, `was able to learn from him who was his attendant,' &c. The question of textual evidence requires further sifting. In support of the statement in the text we may cite Ap. c. Ar. 6, where Ath. is called `one of the ascetics,' which may, but need not, refer to something of the kind."},{"id":"7982dcde61c9","v":"npnf.athanasius.vita_antonii","w":"athanasius.vita_antonii","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":1,"p":[30,30],"r":[19,35],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xix. 35."},{"id":"a10aa8cbc5d8","v":"npnf.athanasius.vita_antonii","w":"athanasius.vita_antonii","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":1,"p":[35,35],"r":[5,26],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings v. 26."},{"id":"4266bc7129d9","v":"npnf.athanasius.vita_antonii","w":"athanasius.vita_antonii","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":1,"p":[35,35],"r":[6,17],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings vi. 17."},{"id":"f528327f08b8","v":"npnf.athanasius.epistulae","w":"athanasius.epistulae","a":"athanasius_von_alexandrien","s":9,"p":[6,6],"r":[6,13,6,17],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"The reference is to 2 Kings vi. 13-17, though `the wilderness' agrees better with the history of Elijah, 1 Kings xix. 4-8."},{"id":"9792a8211abb","v":"anf.irenaeus.adversus_haereses","w":"irenaeus.adversus_haereses","a":"irenaus_von_lyon","s":154,"p":[3,3],"r":[6,6],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings vi. 6."},{"id":"093074fc3d4c","v":"anf.cyprian.de_dominica_oratione","w":"cyprian.de_dominica_oratione","a":"cyprian_von_karthago","s":2,"p":[25,25],"r":[24,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xxiv. 11."},{"id":"48cc81ad901a","v":"anf.cyprian.epistulae","w":"cyprian.epistulae","a":"cyprian_von_karthago","s":75,"p":[8,8],"r":[17,20],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xvii. 20, 21."},{"id":"89d715c20696","v":"npnf.hieronymus.epistulae","w":"hieronymus.epistulae","a":"hieronymus","s":98,"p":[12,12],"r":[4,38],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings 4:38, 2 Kings 4:39, 2 Kings 6:1, 2 Kings 6:2"},{"id":"5fa91d6d1bae","v":"npnf.hieronymus.epistulae","w":"hieronymus.epistulae","a":"hieronymus","s":98,"p":[12,12],"r":[4,39],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings 4:38, 2 Kings 4:39, 2 Kings 6:1, 2 Kings 6:2"},{"id":"c994ecb6ef5c","v":"npnf.hieronymus.epistulae","w":"hieronymus.epistulae","a":"hieronymus","s":98,"p":[12,12],"r":[6,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings 4:38, 2 Kings 4:39, 2 Kings 6:1, 2 Kings 6:2"},{"id":"560c9ded5915","v":"npnf.hieronymus.epistulae","w":"hieronymus.epistulae","a":"hieronymus","s":98,"p":[12,12],"r":[6,2],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings 4:38, 2 Kings 4:39, 2 Kings 6:1, 2 Kings 6:2"},{"id":"71e413902c21","v":"npnf.hieronymus.adversus_iovinianum","w":"hieronymus.adversus_iovinianum","a":"hieronymus","s":1,"p":[38,38],"r":[22,14],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xxii. 14."},{"id":"9f6eb20818c0","v":"npnf.hieronymus.adversus_iovinianum","w":"hieronymus.adversus_iovinianum","a":"hieronymus","s":1,"p":[38,38],"r":[20,18],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xx. 18."},{"id":"04be9a9a1f6c","v":"npnf.hieronymus.adversus_iovinianum","w":"hieronymus.adversus_iovinianum","a":"hieronymus","s":2,"p":[8,8],"r":[23,29],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xxiii. 29 sq. 2 Chron. xxxv. 20 sq."},{"id":"33dfd74a0ebf","v":"npnf.leo.tractatus","w":"leo.tractatus","a":"leo_der_grosse","s":14,"p":[7,7],"r":[6,16],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Cf. 1 John iv. 4, and 2 Kings vi. 16."},{"id":"df0e37cfe8eb","v":"npnf.gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","w":"gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","a":"gregor_der_grosse","s":1,"p":[7,7],"r":[11,3],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xi. 3"},{"id":"5cab472f7c5a","v":"npnf.gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","w":"gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","a":"gregor_der_grosse","s":1,"p":[9,9],"r":[20,13],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xx. 13"},{"id":"e27b39a50cb5","v":"npnf.gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","w":"gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","a":"gregor_der_grosse","s":32,"p":[0,0],"r":[25,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xxv. 10"},{"id":"31d0e7a72609","v":"npnf.kyrill_jer.catecheses","w":"kyrill_jer.catecheses","a":"cyrillus_von_jerusalem","s":3,"p":[17,17],"r":[20,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xx. 1."},{"id":"b093ae861406","v":"npnf.kyrill_jer.catecheses","w":"kyrill_jer.catecheses","a":"cyrillus_von_jerusalem","s":3,"p":[20,20],"r":[25,7],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xxv. 7."},{"id":"77d4f877a80b","v":"npnf.kyrill_jer.catecheses","w":"kyrill_jer.catecheses","a":"cyrillus_von_jerusalem","s":3,"p":[20,20],"r":[24,13],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"R. C. \"But those which had been constructed in the Temple, which were over the mercy-seat of the Ark.\" Besides the two Cherubim of solid gold which Mosesplaced on the two ends of the Mercy-seat (Ex. xxxvii. 7 ff.), Solomon set \"within the oracle\" two Cherubim of olive wood overlaid with gold, ten feet high with outstretched wings overshadowing the Ark (1 Kings vi. 23-26; viii. 6, 7). All these were either carried off or destroyed, when Nebuchadnezzar took away \"all the treasures of the house of the Lord\" and \"cut in pieces all the vessels of gold which Solomon, King of Israel, had made in the Temple of the Lord\" (2 Kings xxiv. 13; 1 Esdras I. 54; 2 Esdras x. 22). The Benedictine editor is concerned because Cyril has paid no attention to the strange fiction in 2 Maccabees ii. 4 that Jeremy the Prophet \"commanded the Tabernacle and the Ark to go with him\" to Mount Horeb, and there hid them, with the Altar of Incense, in a hollow cave, to remain \"unknown until the time that God gathers His people again together.\""},{"id":"486d1aff22a1","v":"npnf.kyrill_jer.catecheses","w":"kyrill_jer.catecheses","a":"cyrillus_von_jerusalem","s":15,"p":[33,33],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 11."},{"id":"4b4afd14669f","v":"npnf.kyrill_jer.catecheses","w":"kyrill_jer.catecheses","a":"cyrillus_von_jerusalem","s":17,"p":[19,19],"r":[5,25],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings v. 25."},{"id":"cc2cc261a1b0","v":"npnf.kyrill_jer.catecheses","w":"kyrill_jer.catecheses","a":"cyrillus_von_jerusalem","s":19,"p":[18,18],"r":[4,34],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 34."},{"id":"3fc45ac46b89","v":"paynesmith.kyrill_alex.in_lucam","w":"kyrill_alex.in_lucam","a":"cyrillus_von_alexandrien","s":6,"p":[49,49],"r":[9,22],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"l This is not a different reading from the Greek text, but the substitution of the customs of the East for those of Greece. In Greece when friends met they embraced one another, and therefore their word for salutation is ἀσπάζομαι, amplecti ; in Rome they said Salve, Be well, whence Saluto: and in the East they asked of one another's peace, 2 Kings ix. 22; whence the phrase in the text. In the present day Orientals greet by saying, Peace be to you; to which the answer is, And to you peace: Cf. also John xx. 26: it is thus that the word for peace, Salaam, has become equivalent with us to salutation."},{"id":"4ee259789484","v":"paynesmith.kyrill_alex.in_lucam","w":"kyrill_alex.in_lucam","a":"cyrillus_von_alexandrien","s":6,"p":[52,52],"r":[4,29],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"o Scarcely any of this part of the commentary has been discovered by Mai; he has however a very short summary of this sermon, in which the Catenist has interpolated an illustration of our Lord's command to the disciples by referring to Elisha's similar instruction to Gehazi to salute no one by the way, when sent to visit the Shunamite's dead son, 2 Kings iv. 29."},{"id":"19198c88fbe3","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":10,"p":[30,30],"r":[20,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xx. 1 et seq."},{"id":"61e771bf3ba7","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":17,"p":[4,4],"r":[25,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xxv. 11."},{"id":"17540e2ca35c","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":22,"p":[7,7],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 11."},{"id":"20d278d28f77","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":23,"p":[10,10],"r":[4,25],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kgs. iv. 25, where the LXX. has \"he contracted himself upon the child until seven times, and the child opened his eyes;\" saying nothing about the sneezing of the child, which the Hebrew and Vulgate mention, while they omit the number in the case of Elisha's similar action. S. Bernard has a curious explanation of the seven sneezes of the child(in Cant. xvi)."},{"id":"9c1d9b49ad2f","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":23,"p":[19,19],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kgs. ii. 11."},{"id":"80355fe878d7","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":25,"p":[75,75],"r":[1,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings. i. 1."},{"id":"4f21c63453e1","v":"npnf.damaskus.expositio_fidei","w":"damaskus.expositio_fidei","a":"johannes_von_damaskus","s":12,"p":[1,1],"r":[19,12],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"See 2 Kings xix. 12; Isai. xxxvii. 12; Ezek. xxvii. 23."},{"id":"b672058d4ca7","v":"npnf.ephraem.hymni_de_nativitate","w":"ephraem.hymni_de_nativitate","a":"ephram_der_syrer","s":1,"p":[9,9],"r":[2,20],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 20."},{"id":"42d03536afc1","v":"npnf.ephraem.hymni_de_nativitate","w":"ephraem.hymni_de_nativitate","a":"ephram_der_syrer","s":8,"p":[9,9],"r":[2,16],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 16"},{"id":"a513d6138dec","v":"npnf.ephraem.sermo_de_domino","w":"ephraem.sermo_de_domino","a":"ephram_der_syrer","s":1,"p":[43,43],"r":[4,26],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 26"},{"id":"b26e9ab58613","v":"npnf.theodoret.historia_ecclesiastica","w":"theodoret.historia_ecclesiastica","a":"theodoret_von_cyrus","s":3,"p":[62,62],"r":[12,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"The form in the text ( glwssokomon ) is rejected by Attic purists, but is used twice by St. John, as well as in the Septuagint. In II. Chron. xxiv. 8 (cf. II. Kings xii. 9) it means a chest. In St. John's Gospel xii. 6 and xiii. 29 it is \"the bag,\" properly (xi. 3) \"box,\" which Judas carried. In the Palatine anthology Nicanor the coffin maker makes these \"glossokoma\" or coffins. Derivatively the word means \"tongue-cases,\" i.e. cases to keep the tongues or reeds of musical instruments. An instance of similar transfer of meaning is our word \"coffin;\" derivatively a wicker basket; - at one time any case or cover, and in Shakespeare (Titus Andronicus Act V. 2, 189) pie crust. Perhaps \"casket,\" which now still holds many things, may one day only hold a corpse.","note":"Stellenangabe der Ausgabe (englische Zählung, umgerechnet)"},{"id":"619f4d5f34d0","v":"npnf.cassian.de_institutis","w":"cassian.de_institutis","a":"johannes_cassian","s":1,"p":[2,2],"r":[1,1,1,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings i. 1-8."},{"id":"8cd39ea2bfbc","v":"npnf.cassian.de_institutis","w":"cassian.de_institutis","a":"johannes_cassian","s":1,"p":[5,5],"r":[6,30],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings vi. 30."},{"id":"a9c2a2709744","v":"npnf.cassian.de_institutis","w":"cassian.de_institutis","a":"johannes_cassian","s":1,"p":[23,23],"r":[4,29],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 29."},{"id":"2121ae52fc4f","v":"npnf.cassian.de_institutis","w":"cassian.de_institutis","a":"johannes_cassian","s":4,"p":[74,74],"r":[9,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Lenticula ; the word used for a cruse of oil in the Vulgate. 1 Sam. x. 1; 2 Kings ix. 1, 3."},{"id":"fc6aa7b4e33c","v":"npnf.cassian.conlationes","w":"cassian.conlationes","a":"johannes_cassian","s":17,"p":[75,75],"r":[20,1,20,6],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xx. 1-6."},{"id":"33f6c0bfbb4d","v":"anf.tertullian.de_oratione","w":"tertullian.de_oratione","a":"tertullian","s":30,"p":[0,0],"r":[4,42,4,44],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings. iv. 42-44."},{"id":"5b225bd132bb","v":"anf.tertullian.de_resurrectione","w":"tertullian.de_resurrectione","a":"tertullian","s":59,"p":[0,0],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Gen. v. 24; 2 Kings ii. 11."},{"id":"a8962d7df8c2","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":2,"p":[28,28],"r":[2,23],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 23, 24. [See notes 4,5,9, following.]"},{"id":"d53ef7238392","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":4,"p":[21,21],"r":[5,9,5,14],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Compare 2 Kings v. 9-14 with Luke iv. 27."},{"id":"e7a3525ebae6","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":4,"p":[27,27],"r":[4,23],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"See 2 Kings iv. 23."},{"id":"ac145f8ddc25","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":4,"p":[46,46],"r":[4,42,4,44],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 42-44."},{"id":"9bbaccf47649","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":4,"p":[50,50],"r":[2,23],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 23,24."},{"id":"f869c59a13e4","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":4,"p":[50,50],"r":[1,9,1,12],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings i. 9-12."},{"id":"6f0bdf6acc3f","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":4,"p":[52,52],"r":[4,29],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"See 2 Kings iv. 29."},{"id":"539875e700e6","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":4,"p":[52,52],"r":[4,26],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings iv. 26. He reads the optative instead of the indicative."},{"id":"bc5ceddf3f45","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":5,"p":[23,23],"r":[19,14],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xix. 14; but the words are, \"quia is sederit ad dexteram templi,\" a sentence which occurs neither in the LXX. nor the original."},{"id":"79f6a0cf80bd","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":5,"p":[27,27],"r":[20,3],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings xx. 3, 5."},{"id":"fbf25b330987","v":"anf.tertullian.adversus_marcionem","w":"tertullian.adversus_marcionem","a":"tertullian","s":5,"p":[29,29],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ii. 11."},{"id":"229b7720c5b1","v":"anf.origenes.contra_celsum","w":"origenes.contra_celsum","a":"origenes","s":1,"p":[77,77],"r":[1,3],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Cf. 2 Kings i. 3."},{"id":"d87d7f489748","v":"anf.origenes.contra_celsum","w":"origenes.contra_celsum","a":"origenes","s":2,"p":[119,119],"r":[4,34],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Cf. 2 Kings iv. 34, 35. [4 Kings, Sept. and Vulg. S.]"},{"id":"a619b637869c","v":"anf.origenes.contra_celsum","w":"origenes.contra_celsum","a":"origenes","s":7,"p":[21,21],"r":[9,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"2 Kings ix. 11."},{"id":"9ff4fa88ec04","v":"anf.origenes.contra_celsum","w":"origenes.contra_celsum","a":"origenes","s":8,"p":[91,91],"r":[4,17],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"[2 Kings iv. 17, 4 Kings, Sept. and Vulg. S.]"},{"id":"621171baeedd","v":"csel.augustinus.de_genesi_ad_litteram","w":"augustinus.de_genesi_ad_litteram","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":18,"p":[7,7],"r":[20,6],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"IV Reg. 20, 6; Es. 38, 5","note":"Stellenangabe im Apparat der Ausgabe (Seite; Absatz ungefähr)"},{"id":"39bf42f8979c","v":"csel.augustinus.de_genesi_ad_litteram","w":"augustinus.de_genesi_ad_litteram","a":"augustinus_von_hippo","s":26,"p":[5,5],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"Matth. 22, 30; Matth. 20, 10; IV Reg. 2, 11; Hebr. 11,40; Gen. 5, 24; Apoc. 11, 3","note":"Stellenangabe im Apparat der Ausgabe (Seite; Absatz ungefähr)"}]}
